ints in 2005  after hurricane katrina had no home  games and their coaching staff and their  players were trying to operate on the  road while worrying about people at home  and look it just didn't go well for the  saints last year but they were the only  ones this year you're going to have to  some extent every team affected in some  way you can't get into a disparity where  if the virus is more prevalent in one  portion of the country and teams are  still playing games versus other  portions of the country but but you're  right the best coaches will take  whatever is dropped in front of them and  mold that into  something that can be used to get his  guys motivated and focused and ready to  perform under any circumstances i mean  we hear coaches say all the time well  we'll go out and play in the parking lot  if we have to i don't know who i'm  imitating there but that's what you know  okay well this is maybe you're going to  be playing in the parking lot this year  who knows where in the hell you're going  to be playing and who knows when you're  going to find out you just got to be  ready to go anytime anyplace anywhere  and you know what in a weird sort of a  way yeah i wish we weren't obviously  dealing with the pandemic but in a weird  sort of a way  it makes this season more compelling if  we go into it not knowing how in the  hell it's going to play out who's going  to get screwed who's going to get the  benefit and how these teams are going to  adapt to all these  constantly changing circumstances  mike you know i i don't say this all too  la i don't say it very loud okay but  it's something that i feel the last five  or six weeks have been the most  interesting time in my 40 years as a  sports writer  because everything is new everything is  weird everything is different  and you know i i sat in on the tampa bay  buccaneers first round of the draft  watching their general manager in his  kids toy room at his home in tampa  i mean that's just we're seeing 